---
/**
 * Our Story — /about/our-story/
 *
 * Sub-page of /about. The client asked for this page to "be like
 * a letter" — simpler than the original build, with no parallax
 * bands, no grey-bg advocacy split, no closing pull-quote, and
 * no ClosingCTA. The page now reads as one long-form white-bg
 * letter followed by a single dark-bg section about John's
 * truck (50/50 text + image, with a 1/1 reading row below).
 *
 * Design DNA (mirrors the rest of the site 1:1):
 *   • about.astro / fire-sprinkler-service-and-systems.astro —
 *     ptbanner (full-bleed hero + breadcrumbs), .section-inner
 *     padding rhythm (1120px max, 110/80px), .section-title
 *     typography, .section-light / .section-dark bg blocks,
 *     diamond-icon eyebrow.
 *   • about.astro §2 .split-grid + .split-image — 50/50 split
 *     with 18px border-radius on the image, reused for the
 *     John's Truck 1/2 + 1/2 row.
 *
 * Sections, in order:
 *   §0. Page-title banner (about.astro §0 pattern)
 *   §1. The Letter — boxed-width white section. Long-form
 *      prose stacked top-to-bottom with two <h4> sub-headings
 *      mid-letter (advocacy + accountability) so the page
 *      reads as a single continuous letter rather than a
 *      series of split panels + parallax bands.
 *   §2. The Story Behind John's Truck — dark-bg section.
 *      Row 1: 50/50 split (1/2 text + 1/2 image, image on
 *      the right so it mirrors the intro's left-aligned text
 *      flow). Row 2: full 1/1 boxed-width text column that
 *      picks up the rest of the truck narrative.
 *   §3. More Than A Truck — A Lasting Legacy. Full-bleed
 *      bg image + dark overlay band with the closing
 *      pull-quote and 3 supporting body paragraphs (the
 *      page's quiet contemplative closer).
 *
 * All copy comes verbatim from
 * reference/content/rrfps-our-story-page.md. Image placeholders
 * use existing public assets — swap to dedicated assets when
 * supplied.
 */

import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Diamond divider icon — used in the double-heading subtitle pattern
// across about.astro, fire-sprinkler-service-and-systems.astro, etc.
const diamondIcon = '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="20" viewBox="0 0 15 20"><path d="M0.660472 19.6191L0.47542 6.53585L13.7437 19.8041L0.660472 19.6191Z"></path><path d="M14.3396 0.939992L0.5246 14.0232L1.25635 0.754941L14.3396 0.939992Z"></path></svg>';

/* §0. PAGE-TITLE BANNER IMAGE */
const bannerImage = '/images/rrfps-our-story-hero.jpg';

/* §2. JOHN'S TRUCK SPLITPANEL — text-left / image-right, dark bg
 * Source: 853x1280 portrait. The truck sits in the lower 40% of
 * the frame (Red Rocks formation dominates the upper portion), so
 * the §2 split uses object-position: center bottom to keep the
 * truck visible after the 1:1 square crop. */
const truckImage = '/images/rrfps-truck.png';

/* §3. LASTING LEGACY — full-bleed bg image + dark overlay band.
 * The classic black Chevy pickup parked in front of the Red
 * Rocks formation. Used as the closing backdrop for the
 * "More Than A Truck — A Lasting Legacy." pull-quote +
 * supporting body copy. The shot has the truck in the
 * lower-center of the frame with the red rock formation
 * rising above it. */
const legacyBg = '/images/rrfps-johns-truck-legacy.jpg';

/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 * PAGE CONTENT — verbatim from
 *   reference/content/rrfps-our-story-page.md
 *
 * All copy now lives in two buckets, both rendered top-to-bottom
 * inside the same .os-intro white-bg letter section:
 *
 *   • introParagraphs — co-founding through to "…handled by a
 *     partner you trust." (9 paragraphs, the existing intro
 *     body).
 *   • letterTailHeadings + letterTailBlocks — the two <h4>
 *     sub-headings (advocacy + accountability) and the
 *     supporting paragraphs that used to live in §2 (parallax
 *     quote), §3a (advocacy intro), and §3b (advocacy image
 *     row). Stacked in the same order the client requested so
 *     the page reads as one letter.
 *
 * The truck section (§2) renders the existing truckParagraphs
 * (4 paragraphs) in the dark 1/2 copy column and truckStory (6
 * paragraphs) in the 1/1 row below it.
 * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
const introParagraphs = [
  'Red Rocks Fire Protection Services was co-founded in 2010 by Brian Kakac and his father, John Kakac, with a shared vision centered on hard work, accountability, family values, and protecting people through dependable life safety services.',
  'John played a foundational role in shaping both the company and the culture it was built upon. Known for his incredible work ethic, commitment to family, and willingness to sacrifice for the people around him, John helped establish the standards and values that continue to guide Red Rocks Fire Protection Services today. His leadership, mentorship, and vision left a lasting impact on both the business and the people within it.',
  'Although John has since passed, his influence remains deeply woven into the foundation of the company. The principles he believed in—integrity, loyalty, discipline, hard work, and taking care of people—continue to shape the way RRFPS operates every day.',
  'Today, Brian Kakac proudly carries that legacy forward as the CEO and Owner of Red Rocks Fire Protection Services, leading the company with the same values and principles his father helped instill from the beginning. Brian’s leadership style reflects a strong commitment to teamwork, mentorship, accountability, and supporting the people around him, both clients and employees alike.',
  'Beyond business, Brian has a strong background in coaching basketball and lacrosse, experiences that reflect many of the same principles his father instilled in him early in life: discipline, teamwork, accountability, leadership, preparation, and showing up for others. That coaching mindset carries directly into the culture at RRFPS, where employees are supported, encouraged, challenged to grow, and treated like family.',
  'From the beginning, the vision for RRFPS was clear: to build a company focused on protecting people, delivered through accountability, reliability, and a higher standard of partnership.',
  'As Brian worked closely with property owners and managers across Colorado, he saw a consistent challenge. Managing life safety systems wasn’t just about inspections and equipment; it meant coordinating multiple vendors, keeping up with evolving fire codes, and carrying the responsibility of compliance across complex properties. Too often, clients were left reacting to issues instead of staying ahead of them.',
  'That realization led to the development of a better approach.',
  'Brian built Red Rocks Fire Protection Services around a One Vendor Life Safety Solution, a model designed to simplify compliance, streamline communication, and provide one trusted partner responsible for all life safety systems within a building.',
] as const;

/* Two <h4> sub-headings + the supporting body blocks that used to
 * live in §2, §3a, and §3b. Rendered in order, each heading
 * followed by its own paragraph(s). The first block (advocacy
 * lede) carries the one paragraph that used to be the
 * advocacyFirstParagraph plus the 4 paragraphs that used to live
 * in the advocacy body. The second block (accountability) carries
 * the single system-list paragraph. */
const letterTailHeadings = [
  'What truly sets Brian and the RRFPS team apart is its commitment to advocacy.',
  'This wasn’t just about convenience; it was about accountability.',
] as const;

const letterTailBlocks = [
  [
    'He dedicates significant time to staying ahead of changing fire codes, legislation, and jurisdictional requirements, ensuring clients are not only compliant, but informed and prepared. More importantly, this knowledge is shared proactively, helping clients avoid issues before they arise and giving them confidence in their life safety systems.',
    'Today, the original vision that Brian and John built together continues to guide the company. Their shared commitment to people, service, accountability, and family remains at the heart of Red Rocks Fire Protection Services.',
    'While John is deeply missed, his legacy lives on through the continued growth of the company, the culture of the team, and the relationships built with clients throughout Colorado. Brian and the entire RRFPS team carry that vision forward every day, and there is great pride in knowing the company continues to honor the foundation John helped create.',
    'Red Rocks Fire Protection Services is known not just for what it does, but for how it serves—showing up reliably, communicating clearly, supporting its employees like family, and standing behind every system, every service, and every commitment made.',
    'Because at the end of the day, this business was built on a simple belief: protecting lives and property should never be reactive; it should be proactive, accountable, and handled by a partner you trust.',
  ],
  [
    'With one team overseeing fire alarm systems, fire sprinkler systems, fire extinguishers, backflow prevention, kitchen hood suppression systems, bi-directional amplification (BDA) systems, area of refuge communication systems, and monitoring, clients no longer have to manage multiple relationships or worry about gaps in coverage. Instead, they gain a coordinated, proactive partner focused on keeping their properties safe and compliant.',
  ],
] as const;

const truckTitle = 'The Story Behind John’s Truck.';

/* Left (text) column of the §2 dark split panel — "For more than
 * ten years…" through "January 2023…". Sets up the dream, the
 * loss, and the auction visit. */
const truckParagraphs = [
  'For more than ten years, my dad, John, talked about finding an old-school pickup truck, restoring it, and putting the Red Rocks Fire Protection name on the doors. He loved classic vehicles and believed a truck like that would represent who we are as a company: hard-working, dependable, and built to last.',
  'Like many great ideas, life got busy, and he never had the opportunity to make it happen.',
  'In 2022, we lost John.',
  'A few months later, in January 2023, I attended an automobile auction. Anyone who has spent time at auctions knows that early Friday mornings can be the best time to find a deal. If a vehicle crosses the block before the crowd arrives, sometimes you get lucky.',
] as const;

/* 1/1 boxed-width reading row that sits below the §2 dark split
 * panel. Holds the 6 paragraphs of the truck narrative that
 * previously lived in their own white-bg section. */
const truckStory = [
  'As I walked through the staging lanes that morning, I spotted an old pickup truck. Something about it immediately reminded me of my dad and the dream he had talked about for so many years.',
  'I took my favorite seat in the auction lane, waited for the truck to come across, and, just as I had hoped, there wasn’t much bidding. I was able to buy it at a great price.',
  'The truck made its way back to Colorado, where we restored it, added Red Rocks Fire Protection graphics to the doors, and gave it the purpose my dad had always envisioned.',
  'Today, you’ll find John’s truck at trade shows, community events, customer visits, and other marketing events. It draws people in because of its classic style, but it means much more to us than that.',
  'At Red Rocks Fire Protection Services, we don’t simply see it as an old truck.',
  'We see it as John’s truck.',
] as const;

/* §3. LASTING LEGACY — closing pull-quote + supporting body. */
const legacyQuote = 'More Than A Truck — A Lasting Legacy.';

/* Body copy that sits beneath the §3 legacy pull-quote (rendered
 * inside the same parallax band). Three "values / One Family /
 * legacy rides with us" paragraphs that read as the closing
 * statement of the page. */
const legacyBody = [
  'For our team, it serves as a daily reminder of where we came from and the values that continue to guide us. It represents the work ethic, integrity, humility, and commitment to people that John demonstrated throughout his life. Those same principles have become the foundation of Red Rocks Fire Protection Services and continue to shape the way we serve our customers, support one another, and do business every day.',
  'One of our core values is One Family, and there is perhaps no better symbol of that than this truck. It reminds us that Red Rocks was built on family, strengthened by relationships, and continues to grow because every employee, customer, and partner becomes part of something bigger than themselves.',
  'John may no longer be with us, but his legacy rides with us everywhere this truck goes. Every mile it travels is a tribute to the dream he never had the chance to complete, and a reminder that we will continue building Red Rocks Fire Protection Services with the same character, heart, and dedication that he inspired from the very beginning.',
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Our Story | Red Rocks Fire Protection Services"
  description="The story of Red Rocks Fire Protection Services — co-founded in 2010 by Brian Kakac and his father John, built on family values, accountability, and a relentless commitment to protecting people."
>
  <div class="av-page our-story">

    {/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         §0. PAGE-TITLE BANNER (about.astro §0 pattern)
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    <header class="ptbanner">
      <div
        class="ptbanner__bg"
        style={`background-image: url('${bannerImage}');`}
        aria-hidden="true"
      ></div>
      <div class="ptbanner__overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="ptbanner__inner">
        <h1 class="ptbanner__title">Our Story.</h1>
        <div class="ptbanner__breadcrumbs">
          <a href="/">Home</a>
          <span class="ptbanner__divider">-</span>
          <a href="/about/">About</a>
          <span class="ptbanner__divider">-</span>
          <span class="ptbanner__current">Our Story</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    {/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         §1. THE LETTER — one long white-bg reading column that
         stacks the 9 intro paragraphs with two mid-letter <h4>
         sub-headings (advocacy + accountability) and their
         supporting body copy. Designed to read as a single
         continuous letter from top to bottom, per client
         request. Mirrors the about.astro .section-inner
         rhythm (1120px max, 110/80px padding).
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    <section class="os-intro section-light">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="section-head">
          <div class="av-dblh__subtitle section-eyebrow">
            <span class="divider-icon" set:html={diamondIcon} />
            <span>The Story of Red Rocks Fire Protection</span>
          </div>
          <h2 class="section-title">A Family Company. A Lasting Mission.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="os-intro__body">
          {introParagraphs.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}

          {/* Mid-letter sub-headings + their supporting body. The
              h4 sits between paragraphs so the body still flows
              as a single letter; the rule for first-of-type
              lead-in only fires on the FIRST <p> in the body
              (the original lead-in), not on the paragraphs
              after the h4. */}
          {letterTailHeadings.map((heading, i) => (
            <>
              <h3 class="os-intro__h4">{heading}</h3>
              {letterTailBlocks[i].map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
            </>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         §2. THE STORY BEHIND JOHN'S TRUCK
         Dark-bg section. Two rows stacked inside the same
         .section-inner (boxed width):
           Row 1 — 50/50 split (text-left + image-right)
                   mirrors the .os-split--reverse pattern from
                   the original page so the eye lands on text
                   first, then the truck photo.
           Row 2 — 1/1 full-width reading column with the rest
                   of the truck narrative (spotting → bidding →
                   restoring → meaning), formatted exactly as it
                   was in the old white-bg §4b section.
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    <section class="os-truck section-dark">
      <div class="section-inner">
        <div class="os-truck__split">
          <div class="os-truck__copy">
            <div class="av-dblh__subtitle section-eyebrow section-eyebrow--light">
              <span class="divider-icon" set:html={diamondIcon} />
              <span>The Story Behind John’s Truck</span>
            </div>
            <h2 class="section-title section-title--light os-truck__title">{truckTitle}</h2>
            <div class="os-truck__body">
              {truckParagraphs.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="os-truck__image">
            <img
              src={truckImage}
              alt="John Kakac’s classic black Chevy pickup — restored with Red Rocks Fire Protection graphics on the door, photographed in front of the Red Rocks formation in Colorado"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* 1/1 boxed-width reading row that sits below the dark
            split, holding the rest of the truck narrative. Kept
            inside the same dark section (not a separate white
            section) per client request. */}
        <div class="os-truck__story">
          {truckStory.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         §3. MORE THAN A TRUCK — A LASTING LEGACY
         Full-bleed bg image (John's classic truck parked in
         front of the Red Rocks formation) with a dark
         gradient overlay and centered white pull-quote +
         3 supporting body paragraphs. Same pattern as the
         original §5 legacy section (a quieter contemplative
         closer before the page ends). The body paragraphs
         render under the centered quote so the closing
         statement reads as one cohesive "letter sign-off".
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
    <section class="os-legacy">
      <div
        class="os-legacy__bg"
        style={`background-image: url('${legacyBg}');`}
        aria-hidden="true"
      ></div>
      <div class="os-legacy__overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <div class="section-inner os-legacy__inner">
        <div class="os-legacy__mark" aria-hidden="true">&ldquo;</div>
        <h2 class="os-legacy__quote">{legacyQuote}</h2>
        <span class="os-legacy__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {legacyBody.map((paragraph) => (
          <p class="os-legacy__body">{paragraph}</p>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   * Shared .section-light / .section-dark / .section-inner /
   * .section-head / .section-title / .section-eyebrow /
   * .av-dblh__subtitle rules come from about.astro. Astro
   * scopes styles per page, so this file owns its own copies
   * that mirror those values exactly.
   * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .section-light { background: #ffffff; color: #0a0a0a; }
  .section-dark { background: #0d0d0b; color: #ffffff; }
  .section-inner {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 1120px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 110px 80px;
  }
  .section-inner--wide { max-width: 1340px; }
  .section-head { max-width: 850px; margin-bottom: 58px; }
  .section-eyebrow { margin-bottom: 23px; display: flex; }
  .section-eyebrow--light { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); }
  .section-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: "Space Grotesk", sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(38px, 5vw, 64px);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #0a0a0a;
  }
  .section-title--light { color: #ffffff; }

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   * §0. PAGE-TITLE BANNER — identical to about.astro §0 (and
   * meet-the-team.astro, fire-sprinkler-service-and-systems.astro)
   * so the hero feels native to the site.
   * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .ptbanner {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    background: #0d0d0b;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .ptbanner__bg {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center 32%;
    z-index: -2;
  }
  .ptbanner__overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(7, 7, 7, 0.46) 0%, rgba(7, 7, 7, 0.58) 100%);
    z-index: -1;
  }
  .ptbanner__inner {
    min-height: 448px;
    max-width: 1120px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    margin-bottom: 60px;
    padding: 136px 80px 36px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 30px;
  }
  .ptbanner__title {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 920px;
    color: #ffffff;
    font-family: "Space Grotesk", sans-serif;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(38px, 5.5vw, 75px);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .ptbanner__breadcrumbs {
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    color: #ffffff;
    font-family: "Space Grotesk", sans-serif;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ptbanner__breadcrumbs a { color: #ffffff; text-decoration: none; }
  .ptbanner__breadcrumbs a:hover { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); }
  .ptbanner__divider { margin-inline: 10px; }
  .ptbanner__current {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   * §1. THE LETTER — long-form white reading column
   * The first paragraph keeps the lead-in style (larger
   * text via .os-intro__body p:first-of-type). Mid-letter
   * <h4> sub-headings sit between paragraphs as
   * sub-section markers, not full section titles. They use
   * the same Space Grotesk 700 family as the rest of the
   * site but at a smaller size than .section-title, with
   * normal case (sentence) so the page reads as a letter
   * with internal sub-headings rather than a marketing
   * page with section banners.
   * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .os-intro .section-title {
    /* Tighter line-height than the about.astro section-title
     * default (1.02) so the 2-line title sits closer to the
     * eyebrow above and the body below — long-form
     * storytelling page, less billboard-y. */
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .os-intro__body p {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    color: #232323;
    font-size: var(--rr-text-body);
    line-height: var(--rr-text-body-lh);
  }
  .os-intro__body p:first-of-type {
    /* First paragraph after the H2 — slightly larger lead-in
     * reads more like an editorial. Mirrors the rhythm of
     * about.astro's `.overview__body` (just at a smaller size). */
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: var(--rr-text-lead);
    line-height: var(--rr-text-lead-lh);
    color: #0a0a0a;
  }
  /* Mid-letter <h4> sub-headings. Smaller + sentence case
   * (not the uppercase section-title treatment) so they read
   * as internal letter sub-headings rather than new page
   * sections. The previous paragraph's bottom margin
   * (paragraph p has margin-top: 22px + body text — the
   * gap from the previous <p> bottom to the h4 top is
   * just 0 + nothing) is too tight visually, so the h4
   * carries a generous top margin of 90px to create the
   * breathing room expected between letter body copy
   * and a sub-heading. 0 below so the supporting paragraph
   * sits flush with the h4. */
  .os-intro__h4 {
    margin: 90px 0 0;
    color: #0a0a0a;
    font-family: "Space Grotesk", sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 1.7vw, 26px);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.28;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-transform: none;
  }

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   * §2. JOHN'S TRUCK — dark section with a 1/2 + 1/2 split
   * row on top and a 1/1 reading row below. All inside the
   * same .section-inner so the two rows share the same
   * boxed width and read as a single dark section.
   * ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  .os-truck__split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 80px;
    align-items: center;
    /* 0 below so the second row (1/1 text) butts up to the
     * split row without extra padding. The 1/1 row carries
     * its own top margin instead. */
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .os-truck__title { white-space: pre-line; }
  .os-truck__body p {
    margin: 22px 0 0;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
    font-size: var(--rr-text-body);
    line-height: var(--rr-text-body-lh);
  }
  .os-truck__image {
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: 18px;
    background: #0d0d0b;
  }
  .os-truck__image img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* Source is 853x1280 portrait — the truck sits in the
     * lower 40% of the source frame, so a center-crop would
     * slice off the wheels. center-bottom keeps the truck
     * (and Red Rocks formation above it) fully visible. */
    object-position: center bottom;
  }

  /* 1/1 reading row below the split. Same boxed-width column
   * (.section-inner caps the width) so the text aligns with
   * the 1/2 split row's copy column above. White-alpha copy
   * on the dark bg so it reads as a continuation of the
   * truck narrative, not a separate white section. The
   * first paragraph uses a larger lead-in style (matches
   * the .os-intro__body lead-in) so the narrative has a
   * clear entry point; subsequent paragraphs use a
   * generous 30px top margin so the 6-paragraph block
   * reads with clear paragraph breaks instead of one
   * continuous wall of text. */
  .os-truck__story {
    margin: 90px 0 0;
    max-width: 920px;
  }
  .os-truck__story p {
    margin: 30px 0 0;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
    font-size: var(--rr-text-body);
    line-height: var(--rr-text-body-lh);
  }
  .os-truck__story p:first-of-type {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: var(--rr-text-lead);
    line-height: var(--rr-text-lead-lh);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  /* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   * §3. MORE THAN A TRUCK — A LASTING LEGACY
   * Full-bleed bg image (the classic truck parked in front
   * of the Red Rocks formation) with a dark gradient overlay
